Quick note first: your notebook still has no sources or notes, so this is a general-law analysis rather than a workspace-cited one — and since the EU payments framework is mid-transition, I'll flag where you should verify the current status of the reform texts.

## Quick Resolution

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| **Source of law (today)** | PSD2 (Directive (EU) 2015/2366) + EMD2 (Directive 2009/110/EC), implemented via the national law of the licensing member state; supplemented by RTS on SCA (Delegated Reg. (EU) 2018/389), Reg. (EU) 2019/518 (FX transparency), Reg. (EU) 2023/1113 (travel rule) |
| **Applicability** | E-money issuance "located within the Union" (PSD2 Art. 1(2)(c)); cross-border transfers = money remittance (PSD2 Annex, point d) |
| **License** | E-money institution (EMI) authorization in one EU member state, passportable EEA-wide |
| **Core obligations** | Safeguarding, SCA, consumer information/liability regime, FX charge caps, fraud/incident reporting, AML, DORA |

## 1. Source of Law

The operative instruments are:

- **PSD2** (Dir. (EU) 2015/2366) — conduct-of-business rules, SCA, liability, transparency for all payment services;
- **EMD2** (Dir. 2009/110/EC) — the prudential and authorization regime for e-money issuers;
- **Supporting regulations** (directly applicable): SCA RTS (EU) 2018/389; own-funds methodology (Del. Reg. (EU) 2019/275); FX disclosure rules (Reg. (EU) 2019/518, since 2020); transfers-of-funds information rules — TFR (EU) 2015/847, replaced by Reg. (EU) 2023/1113 with effect from 30 December 2024;
- **National transposition acts** of the member state of authorization — critically, PSD2 and EMD2 are *directives*, so the binding "source" for the entity is EU text *as transposed* in the home state.

**Incoming:** the **PSD3/PSR package** (politically agreed October 2024) restructures this. The PSR becomes a directly applicable **Regulation** replacing PSD2's Title III (ending national fragmentation), and PSD3 merges EMD2's licensing regime into a single directive. Verify the current formal-adoption status — per the agreed timetable the PSR applies roughly 18 months after entry into force, with PSD3 transposition around 24 months.

## 2. Applicability

**Classification chain:** A wallet storing monetary value as a claim on the issuer, issued on receipt of funds, accepted by third parties = **e-money** (EMD2 Art. 2(2)). Cross-border transfers through it = **money remittance / execution of payment transactions** (PSD2 Annex).

**Territorial scope — the one-leg-in rule:** PSD2 Art. 1(2) applies where only one PSP is in the Union, covering (c) *issuance of payment instruments within the Union*. Because the wallet is issued to EU consumers, the activity is in scope **even though the parent is in Singapore** — and since Singapore is not passportable, the group **must establish an EU entity** to hold the authorization lawfully.

**Exclusions to rule out before concluding:**
- Limited-network / limited-use exclusion (PSD2 Art. 3(2)) — generally unavailable for a general-purpose wallet;
- Technical-service-provider carve-out (PSD2 Art. 3(3)) — unavailable here because the wallet holds funds;
- **Small EMI** registration instead of authorization (EMD2 Art. 9) — possible only while average outstanding e-money stays ≤ €3m;
- Note also that consumer-protection provisions apply as **maximum harmonization** — T&Cs cannot contract out of them, regardless of governing-law clauses.

## 3. License

- **Default: EMI authorization** from the NCA of one member state (chosen by the group — e.g., ACPR, BaFin, CSSF), decided within 3 months of a complete application (EMD2 Art. 9 procedure);
- **Initial capital €350,000** (EMD2 Art. 4), ongoing own funds at least **2% of average outstanding e-money** (EMD2 Art. 5);
- Head office in the authorizing state (EMD2 Art. 8); fit-and-proper shareholder and management tests (EMD2 Arts. 6–7);
- **Passporting:** once authorized, the EU entity serves consumers across the EEA via freedom of establishment and free provision of services (EMD2 Arts. 12–13). The Singapore parent has **no** passport rights;
- **Alternatives:** a full **PI license** (PSD2 Arts. 10–11; €125k or €20k capital) only if no e-money is issued; or a credit institution. If restructured as a pure wallet with third-party acceptance and stored balances, e-money is near-inevitable.

## 4. Obligations

- **Safeguarding:** user funds in segregated, bankruptcy-remote accounts or secure low-risk assets (EMD2 Art. 11);
- **No interest on balances:** EMD2 Art. 11(3) prohibits paying interest or benefits tied to the holding period — a common trap for wallet business models;
- **SCA** with dynamic linking and TRA exemptions (PSD2 Art. 97 + RTS (EU) 2018/389);
- **Transparency:** framework contract and pre-contractual information (PSD2 Arts. 38–42); FX charges must be disclosed at the interbank reference rate, with a **3% conversion-charge cap for non-euro transactions** and dynamic-currency-conversion disclosure (PSD2 Art. 51a via Reg. (EU) 2019/518) — directly relevant to cross-border functionality;
- **Liability/refund:** unauthorized-transaction refunds by end of next business day (Art. 73), €50 cap before notification absent gross negligence (Art. 74), value-date rules for e-money redemption (Art. 83);
- **Reporting:** major operational incidents and fraud statistics (Art. 96); outsourcing of critical functions to the Singapore parent requires NCA notification/approval (Art. 30);
- **AML:** CDD/EDD, plus the **travel rule** under Reg. (EU) 2023/1113 for cross-border transfers (since 30 Dec 2024); AMLA central supervision phases in under the 2024 AML package;
- **DORA** (Reg. (EU) 2022/2554) applies to EMIs since 17 January 2025 — ICT risk management, incident reporting, register of information.

**The one-line answer:** today the product is regulated as e-money issuance plus money remittance under PSD2/EMD2 as transposed in one chosen member state, requiring an EU EMI license with passported EEA coverage — and within roughly the next two years, PSR/PSD3 will convert most of the conduct rules into directly applicable EU regulation.

